<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dirty War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1976">1976</a>–<a href="/years/1983">1983</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dirty War was the campaign of state terror waged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>'s military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> between 1976 and 1983 against left-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>, activists, students and other suspected opponents. Thousands were held in secret detention centres, tortured and killed, becoming the 'disappeared'; human rights groups estimate up to 30,000 victims. Leaders of the juntas were tried after democracy returned in 1983.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dirty-war.jpg" alt="Dirty War" width="320" height="358" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jorge_Rafael_Videla_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1955 the Argentine military removed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-peron">Juan Perón</a> from the presidency in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolucion-libertadora">Revolución Libertadora</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronism">Peronism</a> was then proscribed. Resistance came from workplaces and trade unions, and over the following decade leftist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> groups arose, among them the Uturuncos and the People's Guerrilla Army. Perón died on 1 July 1974 and was succeeded by his vice-president and third wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabel-peron">Isabel Perón</a>. Paramilitary groups, mostly the Triple A death squad run by José López Rega, carried out hundreds of killings in these years. The exact start of the repression is debated: some date it to 1969, others to the Trelew massacre of 1972, the Triple A in 1973 or Isabel Perón's decrees of 1975 against the guerrillas. The military overthrew her on 24 March 1976.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> used the name Dirty War, claiming that a war with different methods was needed to keep social order and eradicate subversives. Human rights groups have rejected the term because it implies a civil war and a justification for the killings. At the 1985 trial the prosecutor Julio Strassera called it a euphemism. The targets were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> and sympathisers, but also students, trade unionists, writers, journalists and artists. Estimates of those killed or disappeared run from 13,000 to 30,000, many of them impossible to document.</p><p>Victims were held incommunicado in clandestine detention centres, at least 340 by later findings, and tortured. About 3,000 deaths are estimated at the Navy Engineering School alone. Some prisoners were drugged and thrown alive from aircraft over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> in what became known as death flights, one of which was organised by the navy captain Adolfo Scilingo. Children born in the camps or taken with their parents were given to other families; the Grandmothers of the Plaza de Mayo had records of 172 at the time of the national inquiry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mothers-of-the-plaza-de-mayo">Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo</a> held their first vigil in 1977.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Economic collapse, public discontent and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falklands-war">Falklands War</a> ended the junta. After the 1983 elections President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raul-alfonsin">Raúl Alfonsín</a> set up the National Commission on the Disappearance of Persons, led by the writer Ernesto Sábato, which documented the disappearance of nearly 9,000 people by name. In 1985 the nine members of the juntas were tried, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-rafael-videla">Jorge Rafael Videla</a> was sentenced to life imprisonment. Nearly 300 people were prosecuted.</p><p>Laws that had granted pardons were repealed by Congress in 2003 and declared unconstitutional by the Supreme Court in 2005, and prosecutions resumed in 2006. In that year 24 March was made a public holiday, the Day of Remembrance for Truth and Justice.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dirty War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A junta led by General Jorge Videla took power and began the '<b>Dirty War</b>'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-24">24 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
